page('waypoints','One proof, several readings','metro',
'''Read down the left track, then up the right track. The combinatorial objects change, but the hard implication stays in the same place. A cut inequality first supplies two disjoint states. The disk identity then makes two regroupings of their signed sum compatible. Fixing the second state cancels every cyclic opposite-edge graph. Grouping by the undirected cycles instead proves that the sum cannot vanish. A surviving forest state supplies a white-face tree, two induced trees, and finally one Hamiltonian cycle. The alternate readings in this atlas explain these stations with heights, integer polynomials, involutions, or turning angles. They are not twenty-eight independent existence proofs. The running example has thirteen vertices in the triangulation and twenty-two in its cubic dual. Pages that change examples say so explicitly.''',
'LIT for the supplied OpenAI proof, Sections 3 to 7. The alternative research routes have mixed review status, recorded on their pages.',
'2 exponential sum; 4 polynomial; 5 heights; 18 primal; 19 recovery.',
'papers/OpenAI-Barnette.pdf, Sections 3 to 7; digestions/route-map/ROUTE-MAP.md.')

page('exponential','Eight terms, two regroupings','terms',
'''The table lists every ordered disjoint pair on the running example. A state number refers to the twelve states in the clock lattice on page 13. The exponent omega is the sum of spoke weights selected by the second state. These rational weights give circulation one around each bounded spoke face. Group first by the second state. The two terms with a cyclic opposite-edge graph have the same exponent and opposite phases, so they disappear. Six forest terms remain. Group instead by the underlying undirected D. Each cycle can be oriented either way, producing a difference of exponentials. The lowest nonzero order is controlled by positive enclosed white area. This is why the displayed finite cancellation illustrates existence rather than merely making a long sum shorter. The computations check all eight terms, not a sample.''',
'LIT: OpenAI Proposition 5.1 and Lemmas 5.2 to 5.3. COMPUTED here for all 8 ordered pairs; the general proof is supplied literature.',
'4 linear weights; 5 heights; 9 incidence cancellation; 12 pair lattice.',
'papers/OpenAI-Barnette.pdf, Section 5; build.py; computed.json.')

page('involutions','Li turns the sum into paired objects','li',
'''Li replaces the analytic sum by signed objects carrying marked intersections. The first involution changes the mate along an opposite-edge cycle while leaving the second state fixed. Its fixed objects are the useful forest states. The second involution follows a different rule: reverse an unmarked D-cycle, or pair successive intersections on a ray from a white face. The remaining objects have a common sign. The picture shows a checked first-involution pair on our running triangulation. The second-involution marks below are computed by exact ray intersections on the cube. On the running triangulation the minimum D-cycle count is two, so the one-mark implementation is insufficient. The laboratory contains a multi-mark extension, but the determinant referee explicitly excluded that implementation from review. The conceptual second involution is in Li's supplied paper; the cube calculation checks the one-mark involution and every sign change.''',
'LIT: Li, Definitions 13 to 23 and Appendix B. COMPUTED: first involution here. COMPUTED cube one-mark involution and signs; multi-mark implementation has not been independently refereed.',
'2 exponential terms; 4 linear weights; 26 open doors.',
'papers/A-Note-On-Barnette.pdf; workings/barnette_lab/barnette_lab/li.py; fable_referee-determinants/REPORT.md.')

page('linear','The same argument with linear weights','linear',
'''Replace each exponential spoke weight by one plus x times a rational circulation weight. Multiply the selected weights for each second state and multiply by the product of local edge signs. Summing over the eight pairs gives the polynomial below the picture. Its coefficients are rational after this substitution; the polynomial in independent spoke variables has integer coefficients. Fixed-state cancellation still removes cyclic states because each cancelled pair selects exactly the same monomial. In the other grouping, each D-cycle contributes a difference of products whose first coefficient is positive white area. All groups with minimum cycle count have the same sign. Therefore the first surviving coefficient cannot cancel. The displayed initial expansion checks this mechanism; the full coefficients are saved alongside it. It does not remove the geometric input: positive circulation is still the reason nonvanishing holds.''',
'PROVED-HERE in the determinant referee, SIGNIFICANCE Section 2; independently AI-refereed reformulation, human review pending. COMPUTED polynomial here.',
'2 exponential terms; 7 white surplus; 10 determinant; 25 Berezin.',
'research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/SIGNIFICANCE.md, Section 2; build.py; computed.json.')

page('heights','Put the height on white faces','height',
'''A state is a matching in the black-centre incidence graph. Subtract a fixed reference matching. The resulting flow has zero divergence, so it is the boundary of an integer face potential. The labels show that potential on the bounded white spoke regions, with the exterior fixed at zero and the lattice minimum subtracted. They are heights of regions, not heights of triangulation vertices. A directed cycle reversal changes every enclosed label by one. Summing these labels gives the matching volume, up to the chosen normalization. The exponential weights read this same volume when all bounded face circulations are one. Thus the analytic exponent has a discrete interpretation. The referee corrected the original wording: paths or a reference state must be fixed before comparing heights. The build solves the boundary equations exactly over the integers for all twelve states.''',
'PROVED-HERE in the combinatorics referee with normalization corrections; independently AI-refereed, human review pending. COMPUTED exact heights on all 12 states.',
'2 exponents; 7 area; 13 clock lattice.',
'research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics/REFEREE.md; build.py; computed.json.')

page('euler','The disk identity as a census','count',
'''The long directed cycle in the running pair encloses five black triangles, six white triangles, and three strictly interior vertices. It has seven boundary edges, two with the black triangle on the inside. These are actual counts produced by cutting the dual adjacency graph at the cycle. The state bijection accounts for the five interior black faces by three interior choices and two inward boundary choices. Thus B equals I plus l. Euler's disk formula gives B plus W equals twice I plus h minus two. Counting signed triangle sides gives three times B minus W equals twice l minus h. Combining the equations forces the signed boundary sum to have magnitude three. The shaded region is the disk away from the root face. Moving the roots into that disk would invalidate the matching count.''',
'LIT: OpenAI Lemma 4.1. COMPUTED: B=5, W=6, I=3, h=7, l=2; all D and directed Q cycles of all pairs also checked.',
'7 white surplus; 8 turning angles; 21 hypotheses.',
'papers/OpenAI-Barnette.pdf, Lemma 4.1; paper/figures/model.py, disk; build.py.')

page('white-surplus','The disk has one extra white face','white',
'''The same disk can be read without orientation. Count the labels in the shaded region: six white faces and five black faces. The useful form of the disk identity is W equals B plus one. It says both that a cycle encloses a white face and that the surplus is rigid. When an edge of D slides onto the two spokes through its black centre, the region between the routes excludes the unchosen corner. After all slides, the enclosed spoke regions correspond to the enclosed white faces. A unit circulation around each such region therefore makes the difference between the two orientations equal six for this cycle. The other D-cycle encloses one white face. Their positive areas are the geometric reason that minimum-cycle contributions to the polynomial have a common nonzero leading coefficient.''',
'PROVED-HERE in both combinatorics and topology referees; independently AI-refereed, human review pending. COMPUTED white areas 6 and 1 on this pair.',
'4 lowest coefficient; 5 heights; 6 disk census; 8 turning.',
'research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics/REWRITTEN-PROP-5-1.md; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-topology/REFEREE.md, part b.')

page('turning','The disk identity as turning number','angle',
'''The labels are signed exterior turns in units of pi over three, computed from the two incident directed boundary edges. Their sum is six on this counterclockwise cycle. Give white corners angle one, the chosen black corner angle five, and the other black corners angle minus one, all in the same units. Each bounded face has angle sum three. Every nonroot vertex has angle sum six because it is chosen exactly once. Euler counting then gives the disk a total boundary turn of six. Negative corner angles are allowed; this is a combinatorial angle assignment, not a Euclidean embedding with ordinary positive angles. The root face is outside the disk, where its exceptional angle sum cannot interfere. The topology referee supplied the boundary flatness argument needed to handle a clockwise cycle as well.''',
'PROVED-HERE in the topology submission, CORRECT after explanatory additions by its independent AI referee. COMPUTED turn labels here; human review pending.',
'6 Euler census; 7 white surplus; 21 hypotheses.',
'research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/TOPOLOGY.md, Section 2; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-topology/REFEREE.md, part a.')

page('incidence','Cancellation is a row dependence','matrix',
'''Fix the cyclic second state from the running example. Each row of this matrix belongs to a bounded black face; its two possible nonzero entries belong to the endpoints of the opposite edge, after root columns are removed. The entries carry the local orientation sign. The vector below the matrix gives an exact nonzero dependence among its rows. It runs around the opposite-edge cycle. Consequently the determinant vanishes. Expanding that determinant chooses one endpoint per row and one occurrence of each nonroot column, precisely the possible mates of the fixed state, with the appropriate signs up to a global factor. The geometric cancellation involution is therefore visible in linear algebra. This reformulation explains why cyclic states vanish, but it does not by itself produce a nonzero determinant for some other state. That is still the positivity step.''',
'PROVED-HERE in the combinatorics referee, exact global-factor correction included. Independently AI-refereed; COMPUTED matrix and null vector here.',
'3 first involution; 10 global determinant; 14 matroids.',
'research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics/REPORT.md; build.py; incidence.json.')

page('determinant','A determinant needs coefficient extraction','determinant',
'''The hexagonal prism is a useful warning. Its dual has three states and two tree matchings. The desired integer polynomial is a coefficient of a determinant whose matrix entries combine local triangle contributions. Each triangle receives its own auxiliary variable. Extracting their squarefree product forces every triangle to be used exactly once. Evaluating all those variables at one discards that condition. Terms that repeat a triangle survive, so the resulting number need not count tree matchings or prove their existence. The displayed counterexample formula was reproduced independently by the determinant referee. Even the valid coefficient norm counts surviving monomials only after nonvanishing has been proved. The running example's fixed-state matrix on page 9 is a different determinant with a different job. These two uses of determinant should not be conflated merely because both involve incidence signs.''',
'PROVED-HERE formulas independently AI-refereed in fable_referee-determinants. REFUTED shortcut: one raw evaluation counts tree matchings. The prism formula is independently reproduced there.',
'4 polynomial; 9 fixed-state matrix; 14 relaxations; 25 Berezin.',
'research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/REFEREE-dimers.md; SIGNIFICANCE.md, Sections 1 and 3.')

page('double-dimers','A doubled spoke is not a D-cycle','double',
'''Overlay two matchings of the incidence graph. If they are disjoint, their union consists of alternating even cycles, and suppressing black centres gives the cycles of D in the triangulation. This is the legitimate double-dimer reading of the pair sum. If the two matchings share an edge, that spoke appears twice. Suppression now produces a backtrack rather than a simple chosen-edge cycle. The disk identity has no disk to count there. One may enlarge the algebra to include doubled edges, but its extra terms must be controlled rather than silently treated as the original pair sum. The picture uses a checked cube state twice and marks the duplicated spokes explicitly. It is a valid doubled matching, deliberately outside the disjoint-pair domain. The determinant referee verified a stronger nine-vertex obstruction to the enlarged shortcut.''',
'PROVED-HERE double-dimer dictionary, independently AI-refereed. REFUTED shortcut with doubled edges. COMPUTED cube doubled matching here; no general cancellation-free proof.',
'2 disjoint pairs; 10 determinant extraction; 21 non-bijective failures.',
'research/2026-11-14_astra_route-dimers/PROOF.md; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/REFEREE-dimers.md.')

page('pair-lattice','Forget orientations, keep the pair lattice','alpha',
'''Each node represents one incidence two-factor, not one ordered pair. The unused corner in every black triangle determines an orientation of the full spoke graph with prescribed outdegrees. Subtracting two such orientations gives integer face potentials, and their componentwise order gives the displayed Hasse diagram. The build enumerates all eight ordered pairs and quotients them into these nodes. A green square means at least one ordered orientation at that node has a forest second state. A ring marks a minimum or maximum. The distinction matters: reversing D-cycles stays inside one node, whereas a lattice move can change the unused corners. The structural referee verified the alpha-orientation correspondence. Large counterexamples refute either specified extreme as a universal choice. A disjunction saying that one of the two extremes always works remains a separate question, not a consequence of distributivity.''',
'PROVED-HERE correspondence independently AI-refereed. COMPUTED complete running-example Hasse diagram. REFUTED individual extreme guarantees; joint-extremes conjecture remains open in the reviewed sources.',
'13 state lattice; 23 canonical choices; 26 open doors.',
'research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure/REFEREE-graph-theory.md; INTEGRATION.md; build.py; computed.json.')

page('clock-lattice','The twelve states under clock moves','clock',
'''Here every node is a single state. Its number is used in the pair table on page 2. The vertical direction increases the sum of the normalized white-face potentials. Edges are covers in their componentwise order, computed by exact boundary equations and transitive reduction. Green squares mark the six tree matchings that occur as second states of forest pairs. Ordinary circles include states with no disjoint mate, so a state lattice is not the pair lattice on the previous page. Rings mark extremal elements. A clock move changes an alternating boundary around a spoke region; the lattice records how local changes compare globally. It does not make acyclicity of the opposite-edge graph monotone. The smallest reviewed counterexample to selecting the highest partnered state has twelve vertices, and reflection reverses the direction. A canonical state requires an additional argument.''',
'COMPUTED complete order on all 12 states and tree markers here. General height reading independently AI-refereed; canonical-extreme shortcuts REFUTED in the combinatorics referee.',
'2 state numbers; 5 height labels; 12 pair lattice; 23 counterexample.',
'research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics/REFEREE.md; build.py; computed.json.')

page('three-matroids','Three constraints, not two','matroids',
'''A corner choice selects an opposite edge. Three conditions must hold together: choose one corner in each black face, choose every nonroot vertex once, and keep the opposite edges acyclic after identifying the roots. The first two are partition constraints; the last is a graphic constraint. Dropping the vertex constraint gives the illustrated hexagonal-prism witness. It has one choice per face and a rooted opposite-edge forest, but repeats a nonroot vertex and misses another. Our enumeration finds four such false common bases among the six choices satisfying the other two constraints. This is why an ordinary two-matroid intersection algorithm does not solve the problem. Dropping the graphic condition instead admits the cyclic state already seen on the running example. The forest is a genuine extra condition, and the signed cancellation is what enforces it.''',
'PROVED-HERE three-matroid description independently AI-refereed. COMPUTED all 6 two-constraint prism bases here, 4 nonstates. No polynomial-time algorithm follows.',
'9 incidence determinant; 15 fractional LP; 23 failed canonical choices.',
'research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure/REFEREE-graph-theory.md; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/REPORT.md; computed.json.')

page('fractional','The natural relaxation has a fractional vertex','lp',
'''Use one variable for each available nonroot corner. The two partition equations require total weight one at each face and each nonroot vertex. For every vertex subset after root identification, a graphic inequality bounds the total weight of opposite edges contained in that subset by its size minus one. Integral feasible points are the desired tree matchings. The picture shows the fractional vertex found on the running example by a fixed-seed search over linear objectives. Solid wedges have weight one, outlined wedges weight one half, and missing wedges weight zero. These are fractional corner weights, not a state or a disjoint pair. The build checks every graphic subset inequality and verifies that the active constraints have full rank. Thus the point is a vertex rather than a convex combination accidentally returned by a solver. This blocks a direct integrality claim for the relaxation.''',
'COMPUTED here with all subset inequalities and full active-rank certificate; exact half values. General relaxation obstruction independently AI-refereed in the structure report.',
'14 three matroids; 26 algorithmic open doors.',
'research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure/REFEREE-graph-theory.md; build.py; computed.json, lp.')

page('rainbow','A white tree with one token per face','rainbow',
'''The green tree crosses precisely the deleted matching spokes. Each deleted spoke belongs to one bounded black face, so that face acts as the edge's token. A tree matching gives a white spanning tree that uses every token once, and every nonroot supplies one deleted spoke. The rainbow language separates spanning-tree connectivity from the matching constraints. An ordinary tree exchange removes one edge and inserts another across its fundamental cut. The right panel records a checked such exchange on this white graph. Treehood and the one-spoke-per-nonroot condition survive, but face-token multiplicities fail, which is why arbitrary basis exchange is insufficient. The topology referee has since proved the rainbow criterion rather than merely observing it on examples. That criterion identifies the right objects. It does not yet provide a local exchange algorithm with a proved termination bound.''',
'Original rainbow dictionary independently AI-refereed. New Theorem R and exchange results in RAINBOW.md are PROVED-HERE but unrefereed. COMPUTED tree and point-preserving exchange here.',
'14 partition constraints; 19 tree/cotree; 26 exchange question.',
'research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-topology/RAINBOW.md; REFEREE.md, part f; build.py.')

page('boundary','What a small cut must remember','boundary',
'''The panels restrict every Hamiltonian partition of the cube's octahedral dual to one triangular and one quadrilateral boundary. Labels are identified under swapping the two tree classes. The three triangle patterns are exactly the choices of the unique monochromatic boundary edge. Across a separating triangle, the pieces can therefore agree by prescribing that edge. Quadrilateral boundaries carry more information; the five displayed label words are computed restrictions in this example. They are not the six piece states of the report, which also record boundary connectivity. A general completeness statement uses the stronger path-avoidance consequence of the supplied proof, and the categorical report still leaves one subcase to finite checking. At length five, the report finds that the realized patterns depend on the piece. Thus gluing cannot be replaced by a single unrestricted boundary rule for all cut lengths. The atlas distinguishes these observed restrictions from a new inductive existence proof.''',
'COMPUTED cube boundary restrictions here. LIT triangular gluing. Categorical 4-cut completeness proof is unrefereed and has a finite-check subcase; 5-cut failure is unrefereed computation.',
'18 cut side; 19 recovery; 24 earlier decomposition methods.',
'papers/OpenAI-Barnette.pdf, Section 7; research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/REPORT.md; computed.json, cube_boundary.')

page('primal','Keep the construction inside G','primal',
'''The picture places the final cycle directly in the cubic graph G. Its vertices sit at triangle centres only so that the example stays recognizable. The primal formalization reads the root as a black vertex and reads a dual disk as the shore of an edge cut away from that vertex. This fixes what inside means without a picture-dependent choice. Face potentials provide one flow on G, and the signed pair argument uses that flow rather than a new weight choice for every cycle. Recovery also takes place in G, turning the selected incidence data into a cycle that avoids the prescribed edge. The Lean dependency report is useful here because it records exactly which planar and cut-side lemmas the proof imports. This page illustrates the recovered object; it does not claim to have independently rebuilt the formalization.''',
'LIT supplied proof; formal dependency description from opus_lean-formalisation is unrefereed research. COMPUTED recovered cycle checked by toolkit and barnette_lab here.',
'6 disk side; 17 cut patterns; 19 recovery.',
'research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/REPORT.md; proofs/primal/; build.py; paper/figures/model.py, recover.')

page('recovery','From a white tree to one Jordan curve','recover',
'''The bold edges P are the selected sides of the triangulation. Black disks and white squares mark their two components. The purple curve is the complementary dual cycle in G. Starting with the forest state, delete its spokes from the incidence graph to obtain a tree. The dual edges across those deleted spokes form the green white-face tree seen on page 16. Root it at the white face adjacent to the prescribed outer edge. Each oriented white-tree edge then selects a side of its black triangle; include the prescribed outer edge as well. The build checks that every triangle has exactly one selected side and that both components of P induce trees. Plane duality makes their interface a single Jordan curve. Following the interface visits each triangle once, so the curve is Hamiltonian in G.''',
'LIT: OpenAI Section 6 and constructive paper recovery criterion. COMPUTED P, inducedness, white tree and Hamiltonian cycle; independent laboratory recovery agrees.',
'16 white tree; 18 primal; 20 produced cycles; 24 Stein equivalence.',
'papers/OpenAI-Barnette.pdf, Section 6; papers/barnette_trees.pdf; build.py; computed.json.')

page('coverage','Two produced cycles, six missed cycles','coverage',
'''Both panels show the same hexagonal prism, drawn as two hexagons with corresponding rungs. Purple edges form a Hamiltonian cycle in each panel. For the fixed root face and prescribed edge, recovery from the two tree matchings produces exactly two of the prism's eight Hamiltonian cycles. The left cycle is produced; the right cycle is absent from that image. Missing an image does not mean missing a Hamiltonian cycle of the graph. It means that this rooted state construction is not surjective. The build enumerates every primal perfect matching, tests which complements are connected, and compares their edge sets with every recovered cycle. This distinction matters when interpreting counts or proposing a uniform Hamiltonian-cycle sampler. A uniform sampler of tree matchings would sample only this rooted image, even if its self-reduction implementation were exact.''',
'COMPUTED all 8 prism cycles and both recovery images here. Injectivity and failure of surjectivity independently AI-refereed in sol_referee-structure/REFEREE-limits.md.',
'10 prism determinant; 19 recovery; 22 family counts.',
'research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure/REFEREE-limits.md; build.py; computed.json, hex_prism.')

page('hypotheses','Four places a hypothesis enters','hypotheses',
'''Read the four panels as failed proof obligations, not as four counterexamples to Hamiltonicity. Bipartiteness supplies alternating face colours and the local signed-side count. Removing it breaks that count, even on a Hamiltonian graph. Three-connectivity ensures the simple dual triangulation and controls small cuts; a two-connected cubic graph can fail the stronger edge-avoidance conclusion. The sphere supplies a disk away from the roots for every relevant cycle. On a torus, a noncontractible cycle has no such disk, so Euler counting cannot force the same surplus. Finally, bijectivity makes every nonroot chosen exactly once. Repeating a chosen vertex destroys the count B equals I plus l. The schematic lists the exact missing object in each case. It deliberately avoids drawing an unchecked toroidal state or presenting failure of this proof as failure of the original conjecture.''',
'PROVED-HERE failure analyses independently AI-refereed in the limits report. Schematic obligations only; no claim of four newly computed graph counterexamples.',
'6 matching census; 8 turning; 11 doubled edges; 18 cut side.',
'research/2026-11-14_astra_limits/REPORT.md; research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure/REFEREE-limits.md.')

page('families','Counts separate existence from abundance','chart',
'''The chart recomputes even prisms with rim length four through twelve. The table directly enumerates width-four nanotubes through three layers. The vertical axis is logarithmic to base two. State counts grow with the rim, the ordered-pair count remains two, and Hamiltonian-cycle counts grow faster than the recovery image. The prism proofs in the family report explain these observations by explicit states. Hexagonal nanotubes behave differently: their transfer matrices remember live matching bits and connectivity partitions along a boundary. Width four keeps only two tree matchings while its state count grows; width six has growing tree-matching counts. Those nanotube tables and their certified recurrences are still unrefereed, so larger-layer claims are excluded from the comparison. A small rooted image can prove existence while becoming a poor model of a typical Hamiltonian cycle. Counting routes must specify which object they count.''',
'COMPUTED prisms p=4,6,8,10,12 and width-four nanotubes L=0,1,2,3 here. General prism formulas and nanotube transfers are PROVED-HERE in an unrefereed family report; no independent review claimed.',
'20 produced versus missed; 26 counting conjectures.',
'research/2026-11-14_sol_counting-families/FAMILIES.md; build.py; prism-counts.json.')

page('canonical','The tempting highest state can be cyclic','canonical',
'''The twelve-vertex witness above is the smallest counterexample reported and corrected by the combinatorics referee for selecting a highest partnered state. It is a different triangulation from the thirteen-vertex running example. Its displayed state is a valid bijection and has a disjoint mate, but the opposite-edge graph contains the dashed cycle on vertices four, five, and ten. A matching extremum therefore does not certify a forest. Reflecting the embedding reverses the orientation-sensitive height direction and supplies the corresponding failure for the lowest choice. The original submission reported a larger first failure for that version; the referee corrected it. This lesson also applies to pair lattices, where specified individual extremes fail on other examples. The picture isolates one obstruction rather than claiming that every conceivable canonical selection rule is impossible. A more informed rule might still work.''',
'REFUTED canonical highest/lowest guarantees, independently AI-refereed with reflection correction. COMPUTED validity and cyclic Q of this 12-vertex witness here.',
'12 pair extremes; 13 state lattice; 26 open canonical rules.',
'research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/data/counterexamples.txt; research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics/REPORT.md; build.py.')

page('earlier','Earlier methods meet the new forest step','older',
'''Stein's two-tree viewpoint already identifies the target: a partition of triangulation vertices into two induced trees. The running picture shows exactly that endpoint. Kelmans's strengthening explains why one asks to avoid a prescribed edge and why decomposition along small cuts matters. Feder and Subi work with quasi spanning trees of faces after a facial colour class is contracted. Those objects provide a different structural route toward the same Hamiltonian interface. The recent older-approaches report warns against identifying their hypotheses with the black-inner separator obstruction: an even-interior condition in one older theorem does not match it. The new paired-state step supplies the missing forest by cancellation and positivity, after an incidence-flow argument supplies pairs. Historical similarity therefore does not make the existence step redundant. This page compares endpoints and obligations; it does not draw an uncomputed Feder-Subi contraction on the running graph.''',
'LIT endpoint equivalence as cited in supplied papers. Older-method comparison is unrefereed research, with explicit original-source limitations; no new equivalence asserted.',
'1 proof stations; 17 decomposition; 19 two-tree endpoint.',
'research/2026-11-14_astra_older-approaches/NOVELTY.md; BRIDGES.md; SOURCES.md; papers/OpenAI-Barnette.pdf, Section 2.')

page('berezin','Grassmann variables package the same coefficient','berezin',
'''An anticommuting variable can force a label to appear exactly once: its square is zero, and integration extracts the term containing every required variable. This packages the face and vertex constraints of the determinant formula. The displayed diagram lists the three filters and points back to the same pair polynomial. It is an algebraic reading of the existing cancellation, not an independent source of positivity. The algebra report also tests whether signs can be removed by changing local gauges. Its counterexamples rule out the proposed universal sign gauge. Likewise, positive squares in a coefficient norm establish nonnegativity, but cannot establish that there is a term to square. The missing step remains nonvanishing in a positive circulation direction. This page includes no invented fermionic state diagram because the supplied route provides a dictionary and obstruction tests, not a new geometric construction on this triangulation.''',
'PROVED-HERE identities and REFUTED gauge shortcuts in sol_route-algebra, unrefereed. The overlapping determinant identity is independently AI-refereed elsewhere.',
'4 polynomial; 9 incidence determinant; 10 coefficient extraction.',
'research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/REPORT.md; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/SIGNIFICANCE.md.')

page('open','The doors that are actually still open','open',
'''The diagram separates three research questions from the established route through the forest step. First, can a local exchange procedure reach a tree matching with a proved efficient bound? Existing reachability statements and marked walks do not provide polynomial time. Second, do the two extremes of the pair alpha-orientation lattice jointly guarantee a tree orientation? Separate extreme guarantees are already false, so the disjunction needs its own proof or counterexample. Third, can contraction lift tree matchings through a controlled injection, or can the exponential lower bound in the minimum D-cycle count be proved? The family calculations provide tests, not proofs of these general claims. Floer terminology supplies another dictionary, but no independent nonvanishing theorem in the reviewed sources. Each door reconnects to a specific checked object in this atlas, so a proposed advance can say which missing implication it supplies.''',
'CONJECTURE and HUNCH only for the new implications. Unrefereed conjecture and induction reports; finite checks are not general guarantees. Established backbone is supplied literature.',
'12 extremes; 14 constraints; 16 exchanges; 22 counts; 25 dictionaries.',
'research/2026-11-14_opus_conjectures/; research/2026-11-14_opus_route-induction/; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-topology/REFEREE.md; sol_counting-families/CONJECTURE-TESTS.md.')

page('morse','Morse cancellation pairs the bad orientations','morse',
'''Fix the cyclic second state again. Its opposite-edge graph has one rootless cycle, so it has exactly two possible mates, corresponding to the two orientations of that cycle. The diagram pairs these two signed terms. More generally, k rootless cycles give a Boolean cube of mates, and reversing the first cycle pairs its vertices without fixed points. The empty cube at k equal to zero contributes a surviving tree state. This is the discrete Morse reading of fixed-state cancellation. It explains which terms disappear, but does not establish that a critical state exists somewhere. That requires the other regrouping and positive circulation. A separate Forman interpretation of the incidence collapse must leave the roots unpaired. The topology referee corrected that point and the direction of the J shift. The running example checks only a one-dimensional cube.''',
'PROVED-HERE dictionary independently AI-refereed after sign and root corrections. COMPUTED complete two-mate cube here. This is not an independent existence proof.',
'3 first involution; 9 row dependence; 13 clock moves; 28 trinity.',
'research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-topology/REFEREE.md, part d; papers/barnette_trees.pdf, Proposition 2.1.')

page('trinity','Three incidence graphs in one triangulation','trinity',
'''Use the cube's octahedral dual to keep this picture readable. Add a black square at each black face centre and a hollow green circle at each white face centre. Join every centre to its three corners, and join adjacent black and white centres across each original side. These are the edges of the second trinity K; the old triangulation edges are omitted. Deleting white centres leaves the black spoke graph, deleting black centres leaves its white counterpart, and deleting original vertices leaves G. The build checks this fourteen-vertex triangulation and all three projections. The categorical report packages Hamiltonian cycles as acyclic triangle factors here, but that equivalence remains unrefereed. The related Floer dictionary reads a rooted degree vector as a supported hypertree. It supplies no new positivity theorem for the simultaneous matching constraints.''',
'COMPUTED K on the cube, with 14 vertices, 36 edges and 24 triangular faces. Triangle-factor reformulation is unrefereed. Floer dictionary independently AI-refereed as a dictionary only.',
'11 incidence matchings; 16 white tree; 19 recovery; 25 algebraic dictionaries.',
'research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/CATEGORICAL.md, Section 2; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-topology/REFEREE.md, part e; build.py.')
